1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,138
A bowl of dal at dinner.

2
00:00:02,138 --> 00:00:03,527
Your skin and bones.

3
00:00:03,527 --> 00:00:05,214
A green leaf in the sun.

4
00:00:05,614 --> 00:00:08,980
All three are packed with the
same kind of big molecule.

5
00:00:09,430 --> 00:00:12,358
Diya's mother says, eat
your dal, it has protein.

6
00:00:12,358 --> 00:00:14,932
So Diya asked: what is a protein, really?

7
00:00:14,932 --> 00:00:16,850
And why must it come from food?

8
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:18,738
Here is the short answer.

9
00:00:18,738 --> 00:00:22,129
A protein is a long chain of
small units called amino acids.

10
00:00:22,129 --> 00:00:25,033
And some of those amino
acids our body cannot make.

11
00:00:25,533 --> 00:00:29,624
By the end, you will know big and
small biomolecules, what a cell is made

12
00:00:29,624 --> 00:00:32,829
of, how a protein is built,
and the jobs proteins do.

13
00:00:33,479 --> 00:00:35,201
First, a quick lab trick.

14
00:00:35,201 --> 00:00:38,057
Grind a piece of tissue
in acid and filter it.

15
00:00:38,407 --> 00:00:41,203
The liquid that passes through
is the acid soluble pool.

16
00:00:41,203 --> 00:00:43,999
Its molecules are small,
from about 18 to 800 daltons.

17
00:00:43,999 --> 00:00:46,412
A dalton is a tiny unit
of mass for molecules.

18
00:00:46,412 --> 00:00:48,526
These small ones are
called micromolecules.

19
00:00:48,976 --> 00:00:51,350
What stays behind is the
acid insoluble fraction.

20
00:00:51,350 --> 00:00:54,320
It holds proteins, nucleic acids,
polysaccharides and lipids.

21
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:57,306
Except for lipids, these weigh
ten thousand daltons and more.

22
00:00:57,306 --> 00:00:59,172
These giants are the biomacromolecules.

23
00:00:59,822 --> 00:01:01,981
Let's zoom into one of these giants.

24
00:01:02,331 --> 00:01:04,485
Inside a cell, proteins are everywhere.

25
00:01:04,485 --> 00:01:06,607
They are the biggest group after water.

26
00:01:07,107 --> 00:01:08,101
Zoom closer.

27
00:01:08,101 --> 00:01:10,692
A protein is a long thread of beads.

28
00:01:10,692 --> 00:01:12,751
Each bead is one amino acid.

29
00:01:13,251 --> 00:01:14,676
Zoom to one bead.

30
00:01:14,676 --> 00:01:18,504
An amino acid has a central
carbon, the alpha carbon.

31
00:01:18,504 --> 00:01:23,598
On it sit a hydrogen, an amino group,
an acid group called carboxyl, and

32
00:01:23,598 --> 00:01:25,565
a variable group called R.

33
00:01:26,215 --> 00:01:27,916
So what is a cell made of?

34
00:01:27,916 --> 00:01:30,050
Let's build the list, step by step.

35
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:31,300
Step one.

36
00:01:31,153 --> 00:01:35,454
Water is the biggest part, seventy
to ninety percent of the cell's mass.

37
00:01:36,204 --> 00:01:37,104
Step two.

38
00:01:36,937 --> 00:01:39,548
Proteins come next,
ten to fifteen percent.

39
00:01:40,298 --> 00:01:41,198
Step three.

40
00:01:41,165 --> 00:01:43,577
Nucleic acids, five to seven percent.

41
00:01:44,327 --> 00:01:45,227
Step four.

42
00:01:44,917 --> 00:01:46,505
Carbohydrates, about three percent.

43
00:01:46,505 --> 00:01:47,787
Lipids, about two percent.

44
00:01:48,537 --> 00:01:49,437
Step five.

45
00:01:49,215 --> 00:01:50,897
Ions make up about one percent.

46
00:01:51,647 --> 00:01:52,547
Remember this.

47
00:01:52,464 --> 00:01:54,395
A protein is a chain of amino acids.

48
00:01:54,995 --> 00:01:57,025
Now watch a protein being built.

49
00:01:57,375 --> 00:02:00,737
Amino acids join one after
another in a straight line.

50
00:02:00,737 --> 00:02:03,701
The links between them
are called peptide bonds.

51
00:02:03,701 --> 00:02:06,381
So a protein is also called a polypeptide.

52
00:02:06,831 --> 00:02:10,991
Proteins use twenty types of amino
acids, like alanine, cysteine, proline,

53
00:02:10,991 --> 00:02:12,315
tryptophan and lysine.

54
00:02:12,315 --> 00:02:15,334
The order of these beads
makes each protein different.

55
00:02:15,984 --> 00:02:17,903
This gives us two new words.

56
00:02:17,903 --> 00:02:19,965
Let's compare them side by side.

57
00:02:20,315 --> 00:02:23,935
A homopolymer repeats only one
type of unit, again and again.

58
00:02:23,935 --> 00:02:25,442
Cellulose is an example.

59
00:02:25,442 --> 00:02:27,190
It is made of glucose only.

60
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:29,883
A heteropolymer uses many types of units.

61
00:02:29,883 --> 00:02:33,998
A protein uses twenty types of amino
acids, so a protein is a heteropolymer.

62
00:02:34,648 --> 00:02:37,185
Now, why must some protein come from food?

63
00:02:37,535 --> 00:02:39,672
Our body can make some amino acids.

64
00:02:39,672 --> 00:02:41,702
These are non-essential amino acids.

65
00:02:41,702 --> 00:02:43,106
Others it cannot make.

66
00:02:43,106 --> 00:02:46,965
These are essential amino acids,
and they must come from our diet.

67
00:02:46,965 --> 00:02:48,510
That is why dal matters.

68
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:51,150
Two proteins hold world records.

69
00:02:51,150 --> 00:02:54,971
Collagen is the most abundant
protein in the animal world.

70
00:02:54,971 --> 00:02:59,638
RuBisCO, an enzyme in green leaves,
is the most abundant protein in the

71
00:02:59,638 --> 00:03:00,830
whole biosphere.

72
00:03:01,430 --> 00:03:02,330
Your turn.

73
00:03:02,135 --> 00:03:05,874
Which amino acids must come from our
food: essential, or non-essential?

74
00:03:05,874 --> 00:03:06,912
Pause and decide.

75
00:03:10,112 --> 00:03:11,455
Essential amino acids!

76
00:03:11,455 --> 00:03:14,571
The body cannot make them,
so food must supply them.

77
00:03:15,221 --> 00:03:16,676
Now, a worked example.

78
00:03:16,676 --> 00:03:18,622
Match six proteins to their jobs.

79
00:03:18,972 --> 00:03:22,011
Collagen is the ground
substance between cells.

80
00:03:22,011 --> 00:03:25,261
And an antibody fights
germs that cause infection.

81
00:03:25,711 --> 00:03:28,472
Insulin is a hormone,
a chemical messenger.

82
00:03:28,472 --> 00:03:32,206
And a receptor senses things
like smell, taste and hormones.

83
00:03:32,656 --> 00:03:35,793
Trypsin is an enzyme
that speeds up digestion.

84
00:03:35,793 --> 00:03:38,505
And GLUT-4 lets glucose enter our cells.

85
00:03:39,155 --> 00:03:40,616
Now, the exam trap.

86
00:03:40,966 --> 00:03:44,354
Many students think lipids are true
macromolecules, because they turn up

87
00:03:44,354 --> 00:03:45,968
in the acid insoluble fraction.

88
00:03:46,368 --> 00:03:48,789
But lipids are small, under
eight hundred daltons.

89
00:03:48,789 --> 00:03:51,916
Grinding breaks cell membranes into
tiny bubbles called vesicles, which

90
00:03:51,916 --> 00:03:52,816
do not dissolve.

91
00:03:52,810 --> 00:03:54,904
So lipids come down
with the big molecules.

92
00:03:55,554 --> 00:03:56,645
Where is this asked?

93
00:03:56,645 --> 00:04:00,019
As statement or match questions on the
protein functions table, homopolymers

94
00:04:00,019 --> 00:04:02,223
and heteropolymers, and
essential amino acids.

95
00:04:02,823 --> 00:04:03,957
Exam level now.

96
00:04:03,957 --> 00:04:06,024
Which pair is matched correctly?

97
00:04:06,024 --> 00:04:07,207
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:12,607 --> 00:04:14,024
The answer is B.

99
00:04:14,024 --> 00:04:16,331
GLUT-4 lets glucose enter cells.

100
00:04:16,831 --> 00:04:18,624
Collagen is not a hormone.

101
00:04:18,624 --> 00:04:21,260
It is the ground substance between cells.

102
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:24,963
Trypsin is an enzyme,
not an infection fighter.

103
00:04:24,963 --> 00:04:26,776
And insulin is a hormone.

104
00:04:27,376 --> 00:04:28,916
Here is your recap.

105
00:04:28,916 --> 00:04:30,498
Screenshot this card.

106
00:04:33,698 --> 00:04:35,884
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:04:36,284 --> 00:04:37,184
One.

108
00:04:36,581 --> 00:04:38,132
Why is a protein called a heteropolymer?

109
00:04:39,032 --> 00:04:39,932
Two.

110
00:04:39,425 --> 00:04:42,776
What is the difference between
essential and non-essential amino acids?

111
00:04:43,676 --> 00:04:44,576
Three.

112
00:04:44,193 --> 00:04:47,068
Which is the most abundant
protein in the biosphere?

113
00:04:48,168 --> 00:04:52,135
Next time: why does a potato slice
turn blue with iodine, while cotton

114
00:04:52,135 --> 00:04:53,035
does not?

115
00:04:52,854 --> 00:04:55,639
We find out in polysaccharides
and nucleic acids.
